---
/**
 * Track 11 (Neural Network Intuition), Lesson 6, illustration 2 of 2.
 *
 * Not every valley is the deepest. A 1D cost curve with two valleys: a
 * shallow one on the left where a ball has come to rest, and a deeper one on
 * the right (the global minimum). A hump between them means the ball, which
 * only ever steps downhill, is stuck in the shallow valley.
 */
const px = (x: number) => 60 + (x / 10) * 640;
const py = (y: number) => 250 - (y / 3.2) * 210;
const cost = (x: number) => 3 - 1.2 * Math.exp(-((x - 3) ** 2) * 0.7) - 2.4 * Math.exp(-((x - 7) ** 2) * 0.9);
let curve = '';
for (let x = 0; x <= 10.0001; x += 0.2) curve += `${x === 0 ? 'M' : 'L'} ${px(x).toFixed(1)} ${py(cost(x)).toFixed(1)} `;
const ballX = px(3);
const ballY = py(cost(3)) - 11;
---

<figure class="cw-illus">
	<svg viewBox="0 0 760 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="nn-lm-title nn-lm-desc">
		<title id="nn-lm-title">Walking downhill can get stuck in a shallow valley</title>
		<desc id="nn-lm-desc">A wavy cost curve with two valleys separated by a hump. The left valley is shallow; the right valley is deeper and is the global minimum. A ball rests at the bottom of the shallow left valley. Because it only ever rolls downhill, it cannot climb over the hump to reach the deeper valley, so it is stuck at a local minimum that is not the best possible setting.</desc>

		<defs>
			<marker id="nn-lm-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" orient="auto"><path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor" /></marker>
		</defs>

		<line x1="50" y1="250" x2="712" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" opacity="0.4" marker-end="url(#nn-lm-arrow)" />
		<line x1="50" y1="258" x2="50" y2="44" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" opacity="0.4" marker-end="url(#nn-lm-arrow)" />
		<text x="700" y="270" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">knob setting</text>
		<text x="44" y="50" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6">cost</text>

		<path d={curve.trim()} fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" opacity="0.85" />

		<circle cx={ballX} cy={ballY} r="11" fill="#7c3aed" opacity="0.9" />
		<circle cx={ballX} cy={ballY} r="11" fill="none" stroke="#7c3aed" stroke-width="2" />

		<text x={ballX} y={ballY - 22} text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#7c3aed">stuck here</text>
		<text x={px(3)} y="272" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.8">local minimum (shallow)</text>
		<text x={px(7)} y={py(cost(7)) + 24} text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.8">global minimum (deepest)</text>
		<text x={px(5)} y={py(cost(5)) - 12} text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-style="italic" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.65">can't climb over</text>
	</svg>
	<figcaption>Walking downhill only ever guarantees you reach the bottom of some valley, not the deepest one. Start in a shallow valley and step only downhill, and you settle there and stop, even though a better setting exists across the hump. So gradient descent finds a good network, usually not a provably best one.</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
	.cw-illus { margin: 1.75rem auto; max-width: 100%; }
	.cw-illus svg { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 720px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto; }
	.cw-illus figcaption { text-align: center; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic; }
</style>
